    "prompt": "Write one program in Rust for the task described below. Its run time and memory use will be measured.\n\nThe program is `main.rs`, the only source file of a Cargo package built with Rust 1.99 (edition 2024) by `cargo build --release --offline`. The package's `Cargo.toml` is fixed: it lists serde (with the derive feature), serde_json, regex and rayon, which are the only crates you may use, and it has no `[profile.release]` section. The program is run as `solution.exe INPUT_PATH` (the task says if it takes more arguments).\n\nYour goals, in order:\n\n1. The program must print the correct output for any valid input as the task defines it, not only for the\n   examples.\n2. Make it as fast as you can. Write it the way a skilled Rust developer would write fast code, using the\n   language's own strengths.\n\nUse a single thread and a single process unless the task says otherwise. Do not precompute or embed answers,\nstart other programs, or read any file other than the input. The program will be run on Windows 11 on a 16-core\nlaptop, on larger inputs of the same kind; the task gives the benchmark size.\n\nReply with the complete contents of `main.rs` in one fenced code block and nothing else. You cannot run or test\nthe code, so check it carefully before you reply.\n\nThe task:\n\n<task>\n# Heat on a grid\n\nSpread heat across a floor plan with walls and fixed heat sources, in exact integer steps.\n\n## Input\n\n```\nH W S\nrow_0\n...\nrow_(H-1)\n```\n\nThe first line gives the number of rows H, the number of columns W and the number of steps S. Then H lines\nof exactly W characters each: `.` is an open cell, `#` is a wall, and a digit `1`-`9` is a heat source. The\ngrid contains at least one open cell.\n\n## Simulation\n\nOpen cells start at value 0. A source with digit d has the fixed value 1000 * d at all times. Walls have no\nvalue.\n\nIn each of the S steps, every open cell gets a new value computed from the values at the start of the step:\n\n```\nnew = (4 * old + n_up + n_down + n_left + n_right) // 8\n```\n\nn_up is the value of the cell above if that cell is open or a source, and the cell's own old value if the\ncell above is a wall or lies outside the grid. n_down, n_left and n_right are defined the same way for the\nother three neighbours. `//` is integer division rounding down. All open cells update at the same time.\nSources and walls never change.\n\n## Output\n\n```\nsum T\nmax V R C\nchecksum K\n```\n\nAfter S steps, T is the sum of the values of all open cells. V is the largest open-cell value, found at row\nR and column C (both 0-based, row 0 at the top); on a tie, report the first such cell in row-major order\n(smallest R, then smallest C). K is the sum over all open cells of `value * (R * W + C + 1)`. All values fit\nin a signed 64-bit integer.\n\n## Benchmark size\n\nH = 400 rows, W = 400 columns and S = 250 steps (about 40 million cell updates). The samples are smaller.\n</task>\n\nExample `s1` input:\n\n<input>\n7 9 4\n#9.9.9#.#\n#####.#..\n..1..#...\n.....#...\n##.###...\n.....#...\n.#2..#..3\n</input>\n\nExpected output:\n\n<output>\nsum 21557\nmax 6151 0 2\nchecksum 369130\n</output>\n\nExample `s2` input:\n\n<input>\n13 13 250\n#9....#......\n.............\n........6....\n......#......\n......#......\n##..#########\n....3.#......\n......#......\n......#......\n......#......\n.........3...\n.............\n......#....1#\n</input>\n\nExpected output:\n\n<output>\nsum 519540\nmax 7440 0 2\nchecksum 33357316\n</output>\n\nExample `s3` input:\n\n<input>\n89 89 250\n#9....#..........#..........#..........#..........#..........#..........#..........#.....\n.....................................................................5...................\n.................8.......................................................................\n......#..........#..........#..........#..........#..........7..........#......2...#.....\n......#..........#5.........#..........#..........#..........#..........#.